# Node.js /hello tutorial (src/nodejs-tutorial) - template for PORT and HOST
# Both are optional: src/server.js applies the default named in each block
# below when the variable is absent or blank, so nothing has to be set.
# HOW THIS TEMPLATE IS CONSUMED: it is a reference and export template, not a
# file the npm scripts read. `npm start` runs `node src/server.js` and
# `npm run dev` runs `node --watch src/server.js`; neither passes --env-file,
# so neither reads .env or .env.example. Both see only variables already
# exported in the shell. There is no `dotenv` dependency.
# To load this whole file, use Node's own loader, which is what replaces a
# `dotenv` dependency here:
#   node --env-file=.env.example src/server.js
# SECURITY NOTE: example values only, never a credential - the /hello endpoint
# requires no authentication. A credential belongs in an untracked .env file.

# Server Port Configuration
# Description: TCP port the HTTP server binds
# Default: 3000
# Validation: trimmed first; blank or absent takes the default. Otherwise it
#   must be base-10 digits over its whole length and inside 1024-65535, which
#   src/server.js enforces before any bind, passing the resolved number to
#   listen(). Non-numeric, fractional, signed, zero and out-of-range values are
#   refused: one stderr message, exit status 1, no default fallback, no bind
# Usage: Read once in src/server.js as process.env.PORT
# Override: export a different PORT for one run when this one is taken; the
#   Troubleshooting section of README.md owns that collision and its fallbacks
# Transcripts: README.md's Run start line and Verify responses are captured on
#   this default; the override ports appear only in its Troubleshooting section
#
# SECURITY: the enforced lower bound keeps the server off the ports that would
# require elevated permission to bind, and rejecting 0 is what stops the kernel
# from handing out an arbitrary port that the startup line would then have to
# guess at
PORT=3000

# Server Host Configuration
# Description: Network interface the HTTP server binds
# Default: 127.0.0.1 (loopback only)
# Validation: trimmed first; blank or absent takes the default. Unlike PORT,
#   which is parsed and range-checked, the only pre-bind check here is a
#   character one - whitespace and control characters are refused, one stderr
#   line and exit status 1. Neither the address format nor local ownership of it
#   is checked, so a value that clears the character check but names no local
#   interface, or a host name that does not resolve, fails at bind time instead
#   (EADDRNOTAVAIL, ENOTFOUND): again one stderr line, exit status 1, never a
#   half-started process. 127.0.0.1 is the only value this tutorial documents
# Usage: Read once in src/server.js as process.env.HOST
# Local development: leave it unset; 127.0.0.1 is the host spelling used by
#   every command, transcript and URL across this tutorial's documentation
#
# SECURITY: binding the loopback interface keeps this tutorial server
# unreachable from the network, which is deliberate rather than incidental
HOST=127.0.0.1
